+# Summary: range matching (`satisfies`, `max_satisfying`)
+
+## What changed
+
+- `depres/ranges.py`: implemented `satisfies(version, range_)` and `max_satisfying(versions, range_)` per `docs/RANGES.md`.
+  - Ranges are parsed into comparator sets joined by `||`. An empty set matches every release.
+  - Supported forms: primitive comparators (`<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `=`, or none), partial versions and X-ranges (`x`, `X`, `*`), hyphen ranges, tilde ranges (`~`), and caret ranges (`^`).
+  - Prerelease rule: a prerelease version matches a set only if some comparator in that set names the same `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` with a prerelease tag.
+  - `max_satisfying` returns the matching input exactly as given (for example `v1.4.0` stays `v1.4.0`), or `None`.
+  - Invalid ranges and invalid versions raise `ValueError`. Compiled ranges are cached with `lru_cache`.
+- `tests/test_ranges_contract.py` (new): 13 tests covering the tables and rules in `docs/RANGES.md`. The suite now has 21 tests in all (8 existing + 13 new). No existing test was edited or removed.
+- `SUMMARY.md`: this file.
+
+Nothing else in the repo was touched. `depres/version.py`, `depres/__init__.py`, `docs/RANGES.md`, and `tests/test_ranges.py` are unchanged.
+
+## Verification
+
+- Before the change: `python -m unittest` ran 8 tests, all erroring with `NotImplementedError`.
+- After the change: `python -m unittest` ran 21 tests, all passing (`OK`).
+- Three of my own new test expectations were wrong on first run. I checked each against the doc and corrected the test, not the code:
+  - `1.0.1` satisfies `1.0.0 || ` because an empty set matches every release.
+  - `1.2.3-alpha` is below `1.2.3-alpha.1`, so it does not satisfy `>=1.2.3-alpha.1 || *`.
+  - `1.2.4-beta.3` does not satisfy `~1.2.3-beta.2`, because the prerelease rule requires a comparator naming `1.2.4`.
+
+## Not verified / unsure
+
+- I did not compare against a reference implementation such as `node-semver`. All expected values come from the doc and my reading of it, so a shared misreading would not be caught.
+- I could not see a CI config in the repo. I ran the command you named (`python -m unittest`), not the CI pipeline itself.
+- Choices the doc does not spell out:
+  - `<*` and `>x` (an operator with an all-wildcard version) match nothing. `<=*`, `>=*`, and `=*` match every release.
+  - `~>` is accepted as an alias for `~`.
+  - A leading `v` is accepted on partial versions too.
+  - Leading zeros in numeric parts (`01.2.3`) are rejected.
+  - Non-string ranges raise `ValueError`.
+- No linter or type checker was run. Only the unittest suite was run.
+- Version inputs are only exercised as `str` and `Version`. Other types (such as bytes) are untested.
